<h2>The conviction under appeal</h2>
<p>The appellant was convicted under Section 376(2)(n) IPC read with Sections 5(1) and 6 of the POCSO Act and sentenced to twenty years rigorous imprisonment with a fine of Rs 20,000 in Special POCSO Case No. 103 of 2024 before the Special Judge, Dhanbad. The prosecution alleged that he forcibly established physical relations with the informant in December 2020 and thereafter on various occasions; she became pregnant, her parents were informed, a settlement followed, and the appellant married her. She gave birth to a son in August 2022 — after which, she alleged, he assaulted her and turned her out of the matrimonial home.</p>

<h2>The bail application</h2>
<p>With the appeal admitted and pending, the appellant sought bail. His counsel pointed to the trial evidence: the victim, examined as PW-1, and her mother, PW-2, had reiterated the FIR narrative — including the settlement, the marriage and the subsequent ouster. On that evidence, counsel argued, the case as it emerged at trial was of a marriage that later broke down, not of the offence as charged; and the appellant had remained on bail throughout the trial without incident. The State opposed.</p>
<p>The Division Bench noted that PW-1's evidence categorically revealed the marriage and the later ouster from the matrimonial house, and held it was inclined to admit the appellant to bail. Bail followed on a bond of Rs 10,000 with two sureties, to the satisfaction of the trial court, for the duration of the appeal. The order is interim: it records no view on the merits of the conviction.</p>

<h2>Situating the order</h2>
<p>Three strands of law intersect here, and it is worth keeping them distinct:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Suspension of sentence is not acquittal.</strong> The twenty-year sentence and the conviction remain; the Bench has only suspended custody while it examines the appeal. The provision at work corresponds to Section 389 CrPC, now Section 430 BNSS, 2023.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Marriage does not compound POCSO.</strong> Courts consistently refuse to quash POCSO proceedings on settlement alone. The victim's age at the time fixes the offence; later events cannot un-commit it.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>But the record is the record.</strong> Where the prosecution's own witnesses depose to a marriage, a child and a matrimonial breakdown, an appellate court may treat continued incarceration during a long appeal queue differently from a contested, hostile-facts case.</div>
</div>

<h2>Practice perspective</h2>
<p>For appellate counsel, the order underlines the value of building the bail application on the trial deposition rather than the FIR: what PW-1 and PW-2 actually said in court — not what the defence hopes to prove — is what moved the Bench. For complainants and the prosecution, it is a reminder that bail pending appeal in such cases is granted on light conditions precisely because the appeal remains fully open; any interference with the victim or breach of conditions can be brought back before the Division Bench.</p>
